WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:53.320
Guy Verhofstadt, welcome to Conflict Zone.

00:00:54.210 --> 00:00:59.079
Let's talk first about the migrants who drowned
last week since that's the crisis of the moment.

00:00:59.079 --> 00:01:06.200
Do you think this is going to be the catalyst
for some new European policies on migration?

00:01:06.200 --> 00:01:13.520
I hope so, because this tragedy in the North
Sea is not the first tragedy, and we have

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:18.720
seen so many casualties, tragedies in the
Mediterranean.

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:26.740
Now this is happening on the North Sea, so
I hope it is a catalyst finally to have a

00:01:26.740 --> 00:01:30.780
common European migration policy.

00:01:30.780 --> 00:01:35.590
Because it's already more than 20 years that
we are talking about a common migration policy.

00:01:35.590 --> 00:01:42.060
And it never happened because a common migration
policy could avoid the tragedies, as we have

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:44.520
seen last week.

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:50.149
France called for urgent talks to discuss
this tragedy, but then it disinvited a British

00:01:50.149 --> 00:01:53.730
minister because he didn't like one of Boris
Johnson's tweets.

00:01:53.730 --> 00:02:00.040
You don't do that if you're serious about
finding solutions, do you?

00:02:00.040 --> 00:02:06.140
I don't think that you start to do such talks
with tweets.

00:02:06.140 --> 00:02:13.990
That's maybe the way Trump did his policies,
but that's not the way we do it inside the

00:02:13.990 --> 00:02:16.910
European, inside the European Union.

00:02:16.910 --> 00:02:24.940
So I think more important is to concentrate
ourselves on what could avoid such tragedies.

00:02:24.940 --> 00:02:33.140
And again, there, a change in the European
migration and asylum policy is absolutely

00:02:33.140 --> 00:02:34.140
key.

00:02:34.140 --> 00:02:41.660
Like, there is also a need for the UK, for
the United Kingdom to understand that they

00:02:41.660 --> 00:02:47.330
are now out of the European Union and they
need to take their own responsibility for

00:02:47.330 --> 00:02:50.030
that migration and asylum policy.

00:02:50.030 --> 00:02:51.030
What did you do...

00:02:51.030 --> 00:02:52.030
I think?

00:02:52.030 --> 00:02:53.030
I think they understand that.

00:02:53.030 --> 00:02:54.030
I think they understand that.

00:02:54.030 --> 00:02:55.520
I'm talking about diplomatic grounds.

00:02:55.520 --> 00:02:56.520
I hope so.

00:02:56.520 --> 00:02:57.520
I'm not.

00:02:57.520 --> 00:03:01.560
I'm not so sure that — maybe you understand
that — I'm not so sure that the British

00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:03.840
government, that Boris Johnson understands
that.

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:07.622
This diplomatic standoff may be great fun
for governments, but the only people who are

00:03:07.622 --> 00:03:09.430
going to get hurt by this are the migrants.

00:03:09.430 --> 00:03:16.950
Isn't it time for everyone to stop playing
games with these people's lives?

00:03:16.950 --> 00:03:23.590
It's the time now to do what most governments,
especially also the British government, but

00:03:23.590 --> 00:03:27.140
also other governments, have always avoided
to do.

00:03:27.140 --> 00:03:32.780
That is to develop a common migration and
asylum policy that is based not on unanimity,

00:03:32.780 --> 00:03:37.340
a ruling on the decision by unanimity, but
on qualified majority.

00:03:37.340 --> 00:03:42.290
Because that's the reason that we don't have,
[for] the last 20 years, a common migration

00:03:42.290 --> 00:03:43.530
and asylum policy.

00:03:43.530 --> 00:03:50.430
It is already since 1999 that we decided that
migration would be a common policy in the

00:03:50.430 --> 00:03:53.270
Union, and since then nothing has happened.

00:03:53.270 --> 00:04:01.740
And the first thing to do is to create and
to establish a common legal migration policy.

00:04:01.740 --> 00:04:08.450
Because many of these tragedies are the consequence
of the fact that we don't have a legal migration

00:04:08.450 --> 00:04:10.340
policy in the Union.

00:04:10.340 --> 00:04:14.950
And the second thing to do is to have common
asylum standards in the Union.

00:04:14.950 --> 00:04:17.480
So there is no shopping anymore possible.

00:04:17.480 --> 00:04:24.170
And the third thing to do is to really better
manage our outside borders of the Union.

00:04:24.170 --> 00:04:27.100
I want to, I want to come on to the outside
borders...

00:04:27.100 --> 00:04:28.100
Unfortunately...

00:04:28.100 --> 00:04:29.970
I want to come on to the outside borders in
a minute.

00:04:29.970 --> 00:04:36.880
But if EU member countries can't agree on
a life-and-death issue like this, what's the

00:04:36.880 --> 00:04:40.900
point of the EU?

00:04:40.900 --> 00:04:45.680
The point of the EU is that only on the EU
level, you can manage this.

00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:46.750
You're seeing it with Britain.

00:04:46.750 --> 00:04:52.020
Britain cannot manage this, although it needs
to ask the European Union to take action.

00:04:52.020 --> 00:04:54.430
Otherwise tragedies will continue.

00:04:54.430 --> 00:04:59.620
Tragedies for people who want to reach and
want to go to the United Kingdom.

00:04:59.620 --> 00:05:04.160
And the point is, our point is, as Europe,
as a European Parliament, we have already

00:05:04.160 --> 00:05:08.810
said for years and I hope that in the next
conference on the future of Europe, we will

00:05:08.810 --> 00:05:12.530
achieve that, that we need to get rid of unanimity.

00:05:12.530 --> 00:05:17.690
The problem is that the fact that you need
the approval of 27 member states before you

00:05:17.690 --> 00:05:18.840
can act.

00:05:18.840 --> 00:05:26.750
And in the meanwhile, such tragedies are happening
because of this impossibility to go forward

00:05:26.750 --> 00:05:33.110
on migration policy because [of] the unanimity
requirement that exists today.

00:05:33.110 --> 00:05:36.890
OK, but at the same time that while this is
going on, the EU...

00:05:36.890 --> 00:05:38.120
It's not the only field.

00:05:38.120 --> 00:05:40.310
Is not the only field.

00:05:40.310 --> 00:05:44.830
The EU is facing a major credibility issue
because it doesn't just sell goods around

00:05:44.830 --> 00:05:47.500
the world, it tries to sell values as well.

00:05:47.500 --> 00:05:53.330
And I'm wondering which values the EU was
selling when Poland was filmed tear gassing

00:05:53.330 --> 00:05:58.230
helpless migrants on the border with Belarus
and showering them with water cannon in freezing

00:05:58.230 --> 00:05:59.230
temperatures?

00:05:59.230 --> 00:06:02.310
Not the greatest advert for EU values, was
it?

00:06:02.310 --> 00:06:06.160
Doesn't it doesn't make you wonder want to
weep?

00:06:06.160 --> 00:06:11.000
No, now, you are not very objective in the
way you put your question, because the problems

00:06:11.000 --> 00:06:16.610
at the Polish border are not a problem caused
by the European Union or caused by Poland.

00:06:16.610 --> 00:06:20.150
They are problems caused by Lukashenko, by
Belarus.

00:06:20.150 --> 00:06:23.570
You think this was an appropriate way to treat
the migrants?

00:06:23.570 --> 00:06:25.889
To tear-gas them?
...to use and abuse migrants.

00:06:25.889 --> 00:06:28.740
Well, and they got abused by Poland, didn't
they.

00:06:28.740 --> 00:06:31.100
Again, that's a problem that has been.

00:06:31.100 --> 00:06:35.950
Now, it's a problem caused by Lukashenko,
so it's not very objective, though, to inverse

00:06:35.950 --> 00:06:42.200
the roles and to do as the migrants on the
border between Belarus and Poland are a problem

00:06:42.200 --> 00:06:45.880
that has been caused by the European Union,
that's not.

00:06:45.880 --> 00:06:47.790
And you know it very well.

00:06:47.790 --> 00:06:54.150
And what we have done in the meanwhile, is
to try to have agreements with those countries

00:06:54.150 --> 00:06:56.170
where these people come from.

00:06:56.170 --> 00:07:02.620
So to avoid that they are abused by a dictatorship
in the neighborhood of the European Union,

00:07:02.620 --> 00:07:04.950
as has been the case the last week and months.

00:07:04.950 --> 00:07:06.730
And so they were abused by other people, then,
weren't they.

00:07:06.730 --> 00:07:10.720
I deplore, as [do] you, the use of violence
and deaths.

00:07:10.720 --> 00:07:18.200
So some of the deals that you've entered into
have resulted, and I'm talking about the particularly

00:07:18.200 --> 00:07:24.250
dirty deal with the Libyan Coast Guard, paid
in effect now by the EU to hunt down migrant

00:07:24.250 --> 00:07:29.770
boats in the Mediterranean and take the people
back home to deal with human rights violations

00:07:29.770 --> 00:07:31.780
are visited on them.

00:07:31.780 --> 00:07:37.090
One thing I guess I have to tell you one thing,
it may be your next time you don't invite

00:07:37.090 --> 00:07:38.860
me to have your interview.

00:07:38.860 --> 00:07:43.590
You invite the commissioner who is responsible
for that because exactly as a member of Parliament,

00:07:43.590 --> 00:07:46.530
I have criticized this towards the European
Commission.

00:07:46.530 --> 00:07:52.150
I am not the member of Parliament who has
to defend the policies of the European Union.

00:07:52.150 --> 00:07:56.980
So next time you take the European Commissioner
responsible for these matters and you ask

00:07:56.980 --> 00:08:01.690
him these questions, I'm as critical as you
on the fact that is happening.

00:08:01.690 --> 00:08:06.160
And that's the reason why I think that European
migration and asylum policy needs to become

00:08:06.160 --> 00:08:13.280
a European Union matter, while it is not today,
because all elements that you have put forward,

00:08:13.280 --> 00:08:16.900
the problems in the North Sea and the tragedy
we have seen in the North Sea, the problems

00:08:16.900 --> 00:08:18.910
at the border between Poland and Belarus.

00:08:18.910 --> 00:08:22.990
The problems in the Mediterranean towards
Libya.

00:08:22.990 --> 00:08:28.690
And people coming from there, and abuse there,
are all the consequence of the fact that our

00:08:28.690 --> 00:08:32.680
member states are responsible for that and
the European Union is not.

00:08:32.680 --> 00:08:35.340
So it's not a question of the European Union.

00:08:35.340 --> 00:08:41.330
It's a question of a lack of European Union,
a lack of European migration and asylum policy.

00:08:41.330 --> 00:08:46.250
But next time, my proposal to you is: invite
the commissioner responsible for it because

00:08:46.250 --> 00:08:51.180
I am as critical towards what is happening
in the Mediterranean and in the North Sea,

00:08:51.180 --> 00:08:52.180
as you do.

00:08:52.180 --> 00:08:55.500
You're critical about it, the media is critical
about it.

00:08:55.500 --> 00:08:59.540
The reports have been coming in and going
to the UN and human rights organizations for

00:08:59.540 --> 00:09:02.690
a long time, and yet nothing gets done about
it.

00:09:02.690 --> 00:09:07.130
Why do you think that is?

00:09:07.130 --> 00:09:12.070
Because like I will explain for the third
time or the fourth time now, that this policies

00:09:12.070 --> 00:09:18.130
for the moment are still policies of the member
states, because there is no unanimity inside

00:09:18.130 --> 00:09:23.990
European Council to change this migration
policies, even at the moment when so many

00:09:23.990 --> 00:09:29.980
people, refugees came from Syria, and the
conflict in Syria towards Europe...

00:09:29.980 --> 00:09:30.980
Yeah...

00:09:30.980 --> 00:09:37.020
Mrs Merkel said, "ok, wir schaffen das, we're
going to pick them, we're going to take them

00:09:37.020 --> 00:09:38.020
inside Germany."

00:09:38.020 --> 00:09:42.200
That was a national measure without consultation
with the others.

00:09:42.200 --> 00:09:50.230
So we need absolutely a radical change in
the way European migration and asylum policy

00:09:50.230 --> 00:09:51.760
is treated in the Union.

00:09:51.760 --> 00:09:53.660
And we have to do it in a common way.

00:09:53.660 --> 00:10:00.650
We have to do it on the European level with
a good border management with common asylum

00:10:00.650 --> 00:10:05.240
and migration conditions and with a legal
migration path.

00:10:05.240 --> 00:10:12.060
Because a lot of the tragedies in the Mediterranean
and in the North Sea are the consequence of

00:10:12.060 --> 00:10:20.480
the absence today of a legal way to come,
to work, to be active as a migrant in Europe.

00:10:20.480 --> 00:10:25.660
You claimed earlier this year that EU integration
has been enormously important, you said, to

00:10:25.660 --> 00:10:30.690
enshrine rules, rights and freedoms into our
societies in Europe.

00:10:30.690 --> 00:10:34.680
But in a number of EU countries, those rules
exist only on paper, don't they?

00:10:34.680 --> 00:10:37.620
And you're powerless, as you said, to do anything
about that.

00:10:37.620 --> 00:10:41.890
Hungary and Poland, for instance, with all
the rule of law issues, they know how to game

00:10:41.890 --> 00:10:43.010
the system, don't they?

00:10:43.010 --> 00:10:47.560
And they can paralyse it if they want to hold
up discussions on the budget, hold up this

00:10:47.560 --> 00:10:48.560
and that.

00:10:48.560 --> 00:10:55.370
So far from enshrining your rules, the EU
is being strangled by them, isn't it?

00:10:55.370 --> 00:10:57.210
No, absolutely not.

00:10:57.210 --> 00:10:59.420
There is a way to tackle this.

00:10:59.420 --> 00:11:06.610
But there is an unwillingness in the European
Council under the heads of states and government

00:11:06.610 --> 00:11:10.540
and under the prime ministers to tackle that
problem.

00:11:10.540 --> 00:11:17.350
What I expect is that with a new German government
arriving, a government of socialists, liberals

00:11:17.350 --> 00:11:22.900
and Greens, that this new government will
be far more critical on this issue, let's

00:11:22.900 --> 00:11:24.450
be honest about it.

00:11:24.450 --> 00:11:27.210
Until now, we didn't tackle the problem in
Hungary.

00:11:27.210 --> 00:11:32.390
We didn't tackle it in Poland because the
European Council, under the leadership of

00:11:32.390 --> 00:11:34.790
Mrs. Merkel, was not capable to do it.

00:11:34.790 --> 00:11:35.790
The parliament asked for it...

00:11:35.790 --> 00:11:36.790
You said they were gutless.

00:11:36.790 --> 00:11:37.790
You said they were gutless.

00:11:37.790 --> 00:11:39.130
They failed to have courage.

00:11:39.130 --> 00:11:40.130
Sorry.

00:11:40.130 --> 00:11:44.500
The commission launched the procedure on it,
and the European Court of Justice also took

00:11:44.500 --> 00:11:45.990
its responsibility.

00:11:45.990 --> 00:11:51.550
The only institution which didn't take its
responsibility was the European Council, and

00:11:51.550 --> 00:11:58.330
I think my expectation is that with the new
German government arriving in power now, this

00:11:58.330 --> 00:12:05.730
will change and we will see really a European
Union who is active using the instruments

00:12:05.730 --> 00:12:12.660
to make sure that the values in the European
Union are also applied in countries like Poland

00:12:12.660 --> 00:12:13.839
and Hungary.

00:12:13.839 --> 00:12:17.120
You've criticized European leaders for lack
of courage.

00:12:17.120 --> 00:12:22.810
You said the, the instruments, for instance,
with, for dealing with rule of law issues

00:12:22.810 --> 00:12:24.880
in Poland and Hungary, existed.

00:12:24.880 --> 00:12:29.360
"The instruments exist," you said, "only the
courage among leaders is failing."

00:12:29.360 --> 00:12:36.100
So why do you think EU leaders are so gutless
on this issue?

00:12:36.100 --> 00:12:41.230
For the simple reason that in the council,
everything is decided between them.

00:12:41.230 --> 00:12:45.890
I have never seen in the European Council,
and I was a long time a member of the European

00:12:45.890 --> 00:12:48.830
Council, one colleague attacking another colleague.

00:12:48.830 --> 00:12:51.270
One colleague criticizing another colleague.

00:12:51.270 --> 00:12:56.459
One colleague sanctioning, I should say, another
colleague around the table.

00:12:56.459 --> 00:12:57.459
That doesn't help.

00:12:57.459 --> 00:12:58.880
That doesn't work in European Council.

00:12:58.880 --> 00:13:03.770
Therefore, I think with the arrival of a new
German government, I think that we will see

00:13:03.770 --> 00:13:08.790
European Council far more critical towards
what is happening in Poland and in Hungary.

00:13:08.790 --> 00:13:15.370
And on top of that, I think that in the future,
we need to maybe to change the instruments

00:13:15.370 --> 00:13:20.810
and change the instruments, that means to
give to the European Court of Justice the

00:13:20.810 --> 00:13:26.070
capability, the competence not only to give
in judgments about what is happening in Poland

00:13:26.070 --> 00:13:30.040
and Hungary, but also to decide on sanctions.

00:13:30.040 --> 00:13:35.230
What is the case in every normal rule of law
system in every country?

00:13:35.230 --> 00:13:40.500
So I think that fundamentally and ultimately,
it will be necessary to give a new competence

00:13:40.500 --> 00:13:45.850
to the European Court of Justice what to give
them the possibility to decide on sanctions

00:13:45.850 --> 00:13:50.740
towards countries that they are not applying
the fundamental values of the Union.

00:13:50.740 --> 00:13:54.730
You mentioned earlier the Conference on the
Future of Europe, which you're co-chairing,

00:13:54.730 --> 00:14:00.950
yet another inquiry into what Europeans want
from their institutions.

00:14:00.950 --> 00:14:07.230
Has there ever been an organization that did
so much navel-gazing and introspection?

00:14:07.230 --> 00:14:13.310
No, it's not, it's not consulting what is,
it's absolutely not that.

00:14:13.310 --> 00:14:18.300
What we are organizing with a conference on
the future of Europe is an unseen exercise

00:14:18.300 --> 00:14:23.779
while we try to combine representative democracy
with participatory democracy.

00:14:23.779 --> 00:14:29.040
The citizens where you're talking about, in
total 800 selected, randomly selected people,

00:14:29.040 --> 00:14:35.240
are not, are not there to give their opinion,
and it's over, no.

00:14:35.240 --> 00:14:38.720
They are participating in the decision-making
process.

00:14:38.720 --> 00:14:45.730
It's for the first time, I think, in a modern
democracy that citizens are directly involved

00:14:45.730 --> 00:14:48.350
in the decision-making process.

00:14:48.350 --> 00:14:49.480
That is what we are doing.

00:14:49.480 --> 00:14:54.980
But, Guy Verhofstadt — not many them, and
not many of them are there, not many of them.

00:14:54.980 --> 00:15:00.010
The numbers taking part on this national digital
platform are so small as to be almost irrelevant.

00:15:00.010 --> 00:15:01.010
I don't, know.

00:15:01.010 --> 00:15:02.010
I don't know.

00:15:02.010 --> 00:15:08.120
There are for the moment, there are, for the
moment, four million people who are on the

00:15:08.120 --> 00:15:13.019
platform who have got access to the digital
platform, the Conference on the Future of

00:15:13.019 --> 00:15:14.019
Europe.

00:15:14.019 --> 00:15:20.110
There are 10 and 10 of several tens of thousands,
who were on daily basis debating on the future

00:15:20.110 --> 00:15:28.250
of the European Union and in top of that,
that have been in a scientific way, 800 people

00:15:28.250 --> 00:15:33.390
selected — and all these people have nothing
to do with European Union — to participate

00:15:33.390 --> 00:15:35.680
in the decision-making process.

00:15:35.680 --> 00:15:44.079
We have seen such what we call Citizens Convention
already in France, already in Ireland, already

00:15:44.079 --> 00:15:45.079
in Germany.

00:15:45.079 --> 00:15:49.960
But it's for the first time that it is organized
on a pan-European level.

00:15:49.960 --> 00:15:54.960
And my expectation of this exercise is not
to listen to the citizens because we know

00:15:54.960 --> 00:15:56.310
what the citizens want.

00:15:56.310 --> 00:16:01.579
We have the Eurobarometer, we have surveys
like it's in the case, also in Britain.

00:16:01.579 --> 00:16:07.850
No: the big difference is that the citizens
are selected will continue to participate

00:16:07.850 --> 00:16:14.710
in not only formulating recommendations, but
also in the answers the, yeah, the politicians

00:16:14.710 --> 00:16:19.550
will give to their wishes and to the recommendations.

00:16:19.550 --> 00:16:25.450
You said: "to regain people's trust in democracy,
we need to fundamentally revisit the way our

00:16:25.450 --> 00:16:30.640
democracies work so they are for and with
the people."

00:16:30.640 --> 00:16:33.510
Instead of that, why not concentrate on results?

00:16:33.510 --> 00:16:38.210
Yes, find a way for 27 disparate countries
to pull together on the major problems that

00:16:38.210 --> 00:16:43.000
are facing them, something that they have
conspicuously failed to do so far.

00:16:43.000 --> 00:16:50.200
Well, for the simple reason that the pressure
that can be created by the participation of

00:16:50.200 --> 00:16:58.450
the citizens will be so huge that I am, I
am confident that we can find two ways to

00:16:58.450 --> 00:17:06.079
break the resistance off of some member states
to go forward inside the European Union.

00:17:06.079 --> 00:17:08.559
We need the power of the people.

00:17:08.559 --> 00:17:15.260
We need the power of the citizens inside this
conference to go through barriers, for example,

00:17:15.260 --> 00:17:17.069
the barrier of unanimity.

00:17:17.069 --> 00:17:23.289
And secondly, we need this also this new type
of democracy because our liberal democracy,

00:17:23.289 --> 00:17:27.809
your liberal democracy, our liberal democracy
is under threat today.

00:17:27.809 --> 00:17:35.070
Under threat from outside the European Union,
China, Russia — but also under threat inside,

00:17:35.070 --> 00:17:40.039
because of the social media, social media
like Facebook are undermining our liberal

00:17:40.039 --> 00:17:41.239
democracy.

00:17:41.239 --> 00:17:47.090
So a direct involvement of the citizens inside
the decision-making process of the European

00:17:47.090 --> 00:17:49.559
Union is really the good answer.

00:17:49.559 --> 00:17:57.039
I think a real answer towards this degrading
new liberal democracy through the social media

00:17:57.039 --> 00:17:58.289
as we see it today.

00:17:58.289 --> 00:17:59.289
Why?

00:17:59.289 --> 00:18:03.639
Why is Facebook undermining your liberal democracy?

00:18:03.639 --> 00:18:06.230
Why?

00:18:06.230 --> 00:18:08.700
Is that is that the....

00:18:08.700 --> 00:18:15.429
I think that's that's obvious, at least everybody
understands that when you go on Facebook and

00:18:15.429 --> 00:18:22.799
you put I don't know what, for example, Chinese
Communist Party on your Facebook, that for

00:18:22.799 --> 00:18:31.129
the next five or 10 years, you will receive
mostly only posts and see posts and messages

00:18:31.129 --> 00:18:32.840
concerning the Chinese Communist Party.

00:18:32.840 --> 00:18:35.580
If that's the only thing you put in, yes.

00:18:35.580 --> 00:18:39.679
But if you put in a whole raft of other things,
you'll get some references to explain.

00:18:39.679 --> 00:18:41.919
Sorry, let me explain.

00:18:41.919 --> 00:18:49.489
The algorithms behind the system of Facebook
are made in such a way that at the end, people

00:18:49.489 --> 00:18:54.669
only see what they think and not the counter
argument.

00:18:54.669 --> 00:19:00.049
And that's a very good model to make profits
in the, in US maybe, in United States, but

00:19:00.049 --> 00:19:05.539
it's a very bad model for the pluralism in
our liberal democracies.

00:19:05.539 --> 00:19:08.149
Let's come back to the EU.

00:19:08.149 --> 00:19:13.679
The question that is often raised about accountability
among the commission.

00:19:13.679 --> 00:19:15.919
Do you believe that this commission is accountable?

00:19:15.919 --> 00:19:19.350
How many of its commissioners ever get sacked?

00:19:19.350 --> 00:19:20.619
Once in its history?

00:19:20.619 --> 00:19:22.389
One since 1958?

00:19:22.389 --> 00:19:25.030
Is there accountability in the commission?

00:19:25.030 --> 00:19:26.640
No, that's not.

00:19:26.640 --> 00:19:27.640
That's not true.

00:19:27.640 --> 00:19:29.289
That's not true what you are saying.

00:19:29.289 --> 00:19:37.220
You forgot to mention the fact that this parliament
at a certain moment has dismissed and put

00:19:37.220 --> 00:19:46.210
home a whole commission once, and also a commission
president in the past.

00:19:46.210 --> 00:19:47.210
Once.

00:19:47.210 --> 00:19:48.210
Well, that's not true.

00:19:48.210 --> 00:19:54.419
So there have been many examples of, of that,
but it's true that we need to increase the

00:19:54.419 --> 00:20:00.249
power of this European Parliament to make
it a real European democracy.

00:20:00.249 --> 00:20:05.580
And the power, additional power that is European
Parliament needs is power on taxation.

00:20:05.580 --> 00:20:08.830
That is something we are missing for the moment.

00:20:08.830 --> 00:20:13.470
And the second thing what we need is the right
of initiative for this European Parliament

00:20:13.470 --> 00:20:16.889
to initiate new proposals.

00:20:16.889 --> 00:20:22.740
So if you tell me that there is not enough
power for European Parliament in controlling

00:20:22.740 --> 00:20:29.049
the commission and in controlling the council
and in developing a number of policies, you're

00:20:29.049 --> 00:20:30.049
right.

00:20:30.049 --> 00:20:31.049
Yes.

00:20:31.049 --> 00:20:35.159
We talked earlier about what you called "failing
courage among Europe's leaders."

00:20:35.159 --> 00:20:40.210
In 2018 you went further and you said "the
union tends to be rather weak in the face

00:20:40.210 --> 00:20:42.039
of global challenges."

00:20:42.039 --> 00:20:47.129
That was pretty much the case, wasn't it,
when Donald Trump was president, allowing

00:20:47.129 --> 00:20:52.679
Europe to be lectured like naughty school
children about NATO funding and who you could

00:20:52.679 --> 00:20:54.529
trade with and who you couldn't?

00:20:54.529 --> 00:20:57.470
Pretty degrading, wasn't it?

00:20:57.470 --> 00:20:58.470
Yeah.

00:20:58.470 --> 00:21:04.470
But yeah, but it didn't fundamentally change
with Biden, Biden or Trump.

00:21:04.470 --> 00:21:11.229
Both are criticizing a European Union for
that and for good reasons, not because there

00:21:11.229 --> 00:21:16.380
is not sufficient funding, but because there
is not sufficient cooperation inside Europe.

00:21:16.380 --> 00:21:22.479
You know that Europe is spending, is spending
more than China on on military expenditures.

00:21:22.479 --> 00:21:28.350
We are spending in Europe — without even
the UK — four times more than the Russians

00:21:28.350 --> 00:21:29.559
are military.

00:21:29.559 --> 00:21:34.440
The problem of our military in Europe is not
the level of spending.

00:21:34.440 --> 00:21:41.249
The problem is that we do it 28 times, 27
times now after the Brexit, and that we duplicate

00:21:41.249 --> 00:21:43.250
everything 27 times.

00:21:43.250 --> 00:21:49.600
We spend more or less 35% of the Americans
on military, and we can only do 10% of the

00:21:49.600 --> 00:21:51.929
operations of the American army.

00:21:51.929 --> 00:21:57.049
What does it mean if you spend 25% of the
Americans get only 10% of the operations?

00:21:57.049 --> 00:22:00.019
That you're three to four times less effective.

00:22:00.019 --> 00:22:02.049
And that's the consequence of duplication.

00:22:02.049 --> 00:22:07.249
So my plea is that the fastest it's possible
we'll create this European defense community

00:22:07.249 --> 00:22:08.990
as a pillar of NATO.

00:22:08.990 --> 00:22:10.809
The European pillar of NATO.

00:22:10.809 --> 00:22:17.179
And that's the real answer to our geopolitical
weakness and not spend a little bit more of

00:22:17.179 --> 00:22:19.940
tax money as we do to it today.

00:22:19.940 --> 00:22:24.059
But do you agree that one of the commissioners,
Thierry Breton, said the other day, that "something

00:22:24.059 --> 00:22:30.779
is broken in trans-Atlantic relations," and
that's neither a reference to Trump nor Biden.

00:22:30.779 --> 00:22:34.979
Is there something that he was talking about
temporary or is the trans-Atlantic alliance

00:22:34.979 --> 00:22:39.109
coming unstuck irrespective of who's in the
White House?

00:22:39.109 --> 00:22:47.170
Now, what, what I see is the absolute need
for reform of the North Atlantic [Treaty]

00:22:47.170 --> 00:22:52.869
Alliance and why I'm saying that when Joe
Biden is coming a few weeks ago to Europe,

00:22:52.869 --> 00:22:55.650
to the NATO summit, he's talking about China.

00:22:55.650 --> 00:22:57.559
He's talking about China.

00:22:57.559 --> 00:22:59.470
That is in the South Pacific, no?

00:22:59.470 --> 00:23:01.139
That's not the North Atlantic.

00:23:01.139 --> 00:23:07.879
That means that our defense issues today are
not related only to the environment in the

00:23:07.879 --> 00:23:13.480
North Atlantic, but are worldwide, and that
there is a need to reform the NATO into a

00:23:13.480 --> 00:23:21.169
global world alliance of democracies and that
on a continental scale, that means that the

00:23:21.169 --> 00:23:28.320
North American continent, the Asian continent,
European continent, has to do a real effort

00:23:28.320 --> 00:23:33.669
to create defense communities to defend our
liberal democracies.

00:23:33.669 --> 00:23:38.470
So the NATO is today still an alliance of
individual countries.

00:23:38.470 --> 00:23:47.139
And what I think is that we need to transform
it into a real alliance of continental defense

00:23:47.139 --> 00:23:49.490
communities defending liberal democracy.

00:23:49.490 --> 00:23:50.869
You want to reform NATO.

00:23:50.869 --> 00:23:54.140
You want to reform rules inside the European
Union.

00:23:54.140 --> 00:23:58.529
You want to change the unanimity rule, you
want a lot of reforms and you want a federal,

00:23:58.529 --> 00:24:02.889
a federal Europe at the end of it, a sort
of one-size-fits-all.

00:24:02.889 --> 00:24:06.019
It's ... what if you don't get any of these
reforms?

00:24:06.019 --> 00:24:10.409
That's absolutely not true, that...

00:24:10.409 --> 00:24:15.820
You have a complete misunderstanding of what
federalism is, because a one fit for all,

00:24:15.820 --> 00:24:18.649
that's not federalism, that's the opposite.

00:24:18.649 --> 00:24:19.649
It's the opposite.

00:24:19.649 --> 00:24:21.399
You see it in the United States of America.

00:24:21.399 --> 00:24:26.789
United States of America, all 50 states with
50 different constitutions, with 50 different

00:24:26.789 --> 00:24:29.039
armies and so on and so on.

00:24:29.039 --> 00:24:31.749
So when I am, I'm a federalist.

00:24:31.749 --> 00:24:32.749
That's true.

00:24:32.749 --> 00:24:37.070
But federalist, for me, means that everything
what you can do on the local level, you do

00:24:37.070 --> 00:24:40.600
on the local level; what you can better do
on a national level, what you do better on

00:24:40.600 --> 00:24:41.710
the national level.

00:24:41.710 --> 00:24:46.970
But where there is an added value on the European
level, that you have to do on the European

00:24:46.970 --> 00:24:53.460
level, and it's the only way for the Europeans
to defend our interests, our values on in

00:24:53.460 --> 00:24:58.399
the new world order that is emerging today
— is emerging — it exists already today.

00:24:58.399 --> 00:25:03.299
There will be a time where it will be the
Chinese who will decide on our standards or

00:25:03.299 --> 00:25:05.409
will be deciding on our way of life.

00:25:05.409 --> 00:25:13.869
And the only way to counter that is to organize
a federal, democratic in diversity, European

00:25:13.869 --> 00:25:15.210
continental European Union.

00:25:15.210 --> 00:25:18.409
Guy Verhofstadt, it's been good to have you
on Conflict Zone, thank you very much.

00:25:18.409 --> 00:25:19.409
OK, bye bye.

00:25:19.409 --> 00:25:20.409
Thank you.

00:25:20.409 --> 00:25:21.409
Thank you very much.

00:25:21.409 --> 00:25:21.909
Bye.

